1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,400
He paid for a pro race car team with payday loan fees.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:06,860
Meet Scott Tucker. So how did his loans work?

3
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:10,480
A payday loan is a tiny loan you pay back on your next payday.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:14,500
Most borrowers were just people trying to cover bills until the paycheck hit.

5
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:17,680
His paperwork said: borrow five hundred, pay back six fifty.

6
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,360
Tap the heart if you'd have signed that.

7
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,020
But the fine print? The loan renewed itself every payday.

8
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:28,940
So every two weeks, they took a hundred fifty in fees, and your five hundred never shrank.

9
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,200
You're basically renting your own debt.

10
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:37,500
One borrower the Justice Department described paid nineteen hundred twenty-five dollars on a five hundred dollar loan.

11
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:41,040
That's around seven hundred percent a year, according to prosecutors.

12
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,160
Send this to the friend who's about to take a payday loan.

13
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:47,400
At least four and a half million people borrowed from him.

14
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,340
More than three and a half billion dollars came in.

15
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:55,980
And when states came after him? He claimed tribes owned the business. Why? States can't sue tribes.

16
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:59,560
Prosecutors called that a sham. So did it work?

17
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:01,940
Follow, 'cause then it all flips.

18
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:06,860
He was arrested in twenty sixteen, and a jury convicted him on all fourteen counts.

19
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:11,040
The judge gave him two hundred months in prison, over sixteen years.

20
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,180
His own lawyer got seven years too.

21
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:16,500
Would you ever take a payday loan? Yes or no.

